% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Space Hunter Lazy Workflow Part 1 -- PIDoc source (DRAFT)
%
% \script must be the script's MENU name (last part of the menu path
% in #feature-id), not the ID: PixInsight's help button looks up
% doc/scripts/<menu name>/<menu name>.html. Tested in PI 2026-10-12.
%    #feature-id SHLazyWorkflowPart1 : Space_Hunter > SH Lazy Workflow Part 1
% and the dialog's documentation button calls
%    Dialog.browseScriptDocumentation( "SH Lazy Workflow Part 1" )
% Written against script v2.5 (ScriptsTesting/Final Tests), 2026-10-11.
% MGC scale factors, model panels and re-run notes updated for v2.7.4,
% 2026-11-01. Profile folder / save prompt and GC previews for v2.7.7,
% 2026-11-02.
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\script {SH Lazy Workflow Part 1}

\keywords {
   Space Hunter, OSC, ImageSolver, blind solve, SPFC, flux calibration, RGB, dual-narrowband,
   Ha/OIII separation, MultiscaleGradientCorrection, GradientCorrection,
   DynamicBackgroundExtraction
}

\author {
   Georg G. Albrecht
}

\copyright {
   2026 Georg G. Albrecht. MIT License.
}

\brief {
   Automates the linear OSC workflow from plate solving to background extraction: smart plate solve, SPFC, previews and MGC, with GC and DBE as alternatives.
}

\description {

   The Space Hunter Lazy Workflow automates the repetitive, non-artistic steps
   of processing a linear OSC image, so you only make the decisions that need
   your judgment. It comes in two parts:

   \list[spaced] {
      { \s {Part 1} (this script) takes a cropped image from plate solving to
        background extraction. }
      { \s {Part 2} (\sref {SH Lazy Workflow Part 2} {SH Lazy Workflow Part 2})
        continues from the background-extracted image to a star-separated
        image that is ready to stretch. }
   }

   Every step can be switched on or off. If you prefer GradientCorrection (GC)
   or DynamicBackgroundExtraction (DBE) to MultiscaleGradientCorrection (MGC),
   you can switch with one checkbox.

   \s {Part 1 sequence:} Plate Solve &rarr; (Ha/OIII separation) &rarr; SPFC
   &rarr; Previews &rarr; MGC &rarr; choose and apply a background model.
   Alternatives: GC or DBE.
}

\section { Requirements and License } {
   \include SH_Common.pidoc
}

\usage {

   \subsection { \label dialog The Dialog } {

      \figure[numbered: part1_dialog] {
         \figtag \s { Part 1 dialog }
         \imageselect[menupos:right, imageWidth:400px] {
            images/Part1_0.png { Overview }
            images/Part1_1.png { Default settings }
            images/Part1_2.png { Dual-band filter: narrowband mode, Ha/OIII
                                 separation and MGC filter selection }
         }
      }

      Each workflow step is a collapsible section with a checkbox. By default
      Plate Solve, SPFC, Previews and MGC are on; GC and DBE are off.

      \table[caption, header, numbered: part1_defaults] {
         { \tbltag Workflow steps, defaults and dependencies }
         { {Step} {Default} {Dependencies} }
         { { \lref step_solve {1 Plate Solve} (with blind solve) } {On}
           { Can run alone. Blind solve can only be on while Plate Solve is on. } }
         { { \lref separation {2a Ha/OIII separation} } {Off}
           { Only in narrowband filters mode. Runs just before SPFC. } }
         { { \lref step_spfc {2b SPFC} } {On}
           { Turning it on also turns on Plate Solve and blind solve. } }
         { { \lref step_previews {3 Previews (MGC only)} } {On, 4 previews}
           { Turned on by MGC; turned off by GC and DBE. } }
         { { \lref step_mgc {4 MGC} } {On}
           { Turning it on also turns on SPFC and Previews (and so Plate Solve). } }
         { { \lref step_gc {5 GC} } {Off}
           { Turning it on turns off SPFC, Previews, MGC and DBE, and turns on
             Plate Solve. GC makes its own two previews. } }
         { { \lref step_dbe {6 DBE} } {Off}
           { Turning it on turns off SPFC, Previews, MGC and GC, and turns on
             Plate Solve. } }
      }
   }

   \subsection { \label step_solve Step 1 --- Smart Plate Solve } {

      Checks whether the image already has an astrometric solution. If it
      does, the step is skipped. If not, the script reads the FITS keywords:

      \list[spaced] {
         { If coordinates and image scale are present, the script solves the
           image with PixInsight's ImageSolver engine. No dialog opens. }
         { If they are missing and \s {Blind solve (if needed)} is checked
           (the default), ImageSolver finds the position and scale from the
           stars alone. It also does this when solving with the keywords
           fails. }
      }

      Blind solving needs the Gaia astrometric index file, set in
      \m {Process > Astrometry > Gaia} preferences (see
      \lref requirements Requirements). The Plate Solve section of the
      dialog shows the index file in use, or \e {No astrometric index set}.
      Without it, images that lack coordinates or image scale are not
      solved, and the console explains what to set up.

      If solving fails, the console says so. Solve the image manually with
      \m {Script > Image Analysis > ImageSolver} and run Part 1 again.

      SPFC and MGC need this astrometric solution, and so does SPCC in Part 2.
   }

   \subsection { \label step_spfc Step 2b --- SPFC (SpectrophotometricFluxCalibration) } {

      Applies spectrophotometric flux calibration with the camera and filter
      curves selected in the dialog. This linearises the colour response of
      the image, and MGC needs it.

      \definition {
         { Camera / Sensor } { The camera's QE curve. \e {Ideal QE curve} if
                               yours isn't listed. }
         { Gray filter }     { The luminance (L) filter curve. }
         { RGB filters }     { The colour filter set, e.g. the camera's own
                               Bayer filters. }
         { Generate graphs } { Lets SPFC show its calibration graphs. }
         { Narrowband filters mode } { For a dual-band filter (e.g. Optolong
                               L-eXtreme or L-Ultimate, Antlia ALP-T, Askar
                               ColorMagic D1). The filter lists are replaced by
                               wavelength and bandwidth (nm) for red, green
                               and blue, e.g. 656.3 / 500.7 / 500.7 for
                               Ha/OIII/OIII. }
      }

      \subsection { \label separation Step 2a --- Ha/OIII Separation (dual-band only) } {

         \center {
            \image[width:560px] images/Part1_4.png
         }

         \s {Why.} With a dual-band filter on an OSC sensor, Ha light also
         reaches the green and blue pixels. So G and B are not pure OIII, and
         SPFC and MGC (with an OIII reference) work on the wrong signal. In
         an Ha-rich field the green channel can look like an Ha image.

         \s {What it does.} Before SPFC, the script subtracts a fraction of
         the red (Ha) channel's signal above its median from G and B with
         PixelMath. Red stays unchanged:

         \code[lang:pmath] { #:
R:  $T
G:  $T - ( $T[0] - med( $T[0] ) ) * kG
B:  $T - ( $T[0] - med( $T[0] ) ) * kB
         :# }

         The method is from the PixInsight video
         \e {MultiscaleGradientCorrection --- Dual-Band OSC Images (II)}
         \ref video_mgc_dnb.

         \s {When it runs.} Only when SPFC, \s {Narrowband filters mode}
         and \s {Ha/OIII separation before SPFC} are all checked. It runs
         once per image: it is skipped if the image is not a colour image,
         if both factors are 0, if it was already applied, or if SPFC was
         already applied (undo SPFC in History Explorer first). If the
         separation itself fails, the script stops.

         \subsection { \label sep_factors Finding the Factors } {

            The factors kG and kB depend on your camera and filter, not on the
            target, so you find them once.

            \list[ordered, spaced] {
               { Click \s {PixelMath &amp; exit}. The dialog closes without
                 processing and PixelMath opens with the separation
                 expressions (0.5 if both factors are 0). }
               { Create a preview over an area with Ha nebulosity and apply
                 PixelMath to it. Look at G and B separately. }
               { Lower the factor (Undo between tries) until the Ha
                 structures just disappear in G and B, not until they turn
                 dark. Real OIII (for example the Crescent Nebula's shell)
                 stays. }
               { Run Part 1 again, enter kG and kB, and click
                 \s {Save profile...}. }
            }

            Example: IMX571 sensor with an Antlia ALP-T filter,
            kG = 0.09, kB = 0.03. Checked on four different targets with this
            camera and filter, the factors agreed within &plusmn;0.01. Find
            them again only when you change camera or filter.

            \figure[numbered: pixelmath] {
               \figtag \s { The separation expressions in PixelMath }
               \imageselect[menupos:right, imageWidth:520px] {
                  images/Part1_PixelMath_1.png { R/K: unchanged }
                  images/Part1_PixelMath_2.png { G: kG }
                  images/Part1_PixelMath_3.png { B: kB }
               }
            }
         }

         \subsection { \label sep_profiles Profiles } {

            Keep one profile per camera and filter combination:

            \list[spaced] {
               { Choose a \s {Profile folder} once (folder button). The
                 folder is remembered on this computer (process icons don't
                 carry it). }
               { \s {Profile} lists the folder's \c .txt files. Choosing one
                 fills in its factors; it is re-read from the file each time
                 the script starts. }
               { \s {Save profile...} writes the current kG and kB to a new
                 file; if the file already exists, the script asks before
                 replacing it. Typing a factor switches the profile to
                 \e {(manual values)}; save it to keep it. }
               { You can also save PixelMath's \s {Instance Source Code}
                 (the square button at the bottom right of PixelMath) as a
                 \c .txt file in the folder. }
            }

            Profile files are only read for their two numbers. They are
            never executed.

            \figure[numbered: pixelmath_source] {
               \figtag \s { PixelMath instance source code as a profile }
               \imageselect[menupos:right, imageWidth:520px] {
                  images/Part1_PixelMath_4.png { Instance Source Code button }
                  images/Part1_PixelMath_5.png { The source code to save }
               }
            }
         }
      }
   }

   \subsection { \label step_previews Step 3 --- Create Previews (MGC only) } {

      Creates 1 to 4 full-size previews (default 4), one for each MGC
      gradient scale. Existing previews are deleted first. The names depend
      on what runs:

      \table[header, unnumbered] {
         { {Situation} {Preview names} }
         { {Default run (with MGC)} { \c BM_768, \c BM_1024, ...: one
                                      background model per gradient scale.
                                      The same scale twice gives
                                      \c BM_1024_1 (as in +Previews). } }
         { {MGC with \lref scale_factors {Scale factors} \e Automatic}
                                    { \c BM_768_autoSF, \c BM_1024_autoSF, ... }
         }
         { {MGC with \lref scale_factors {Scale factors} \e Both}
                                    { Two per scale: \c BM_768 and
                                      \c BM_768_autoSF, \c BM_1024 and
                                      \c BM_1024_autoSF, ... (8 previews for
                                      4 scales). } }
         { {Previews run alone}     { \c Preview, \c Preview1, ... } }
         { {GC selected}            { GC creates its own \c GC_ and \c GC_1. } }
         { {DBE selected}           { No previews are created. } }
      }

      To add more previews later, or to use previews on their own, use
      \lref bonus_scripts {+Previews}.
   }

   \subsection { \label step_mgc Step 4 --- MGC (MultiscaleGradientCorrection) } {

      Runs MGC on each preview with its own gradient scale (default 768,
      1024, 2048 and 3072), using the MARS database as reference. Each
      preview then shows its gradient model; the script pauses 3 seconds on
      each so you can follow along.

      By default MGC uses L/R/G/B. For a dual-band filter, check
      \s {Filter selection} and choose L, Ha, OIII, OIII, together with
      \lref step_spfc {narrowband filters mode} in SPFC and, ideally, the
      \lref separation {Ha/OIII separation} (\figref part1_dialog).

      \subsection { \label gradient_scale Choosing Gradient Scales } {

         Start with 1024. With a 2&times; drizzled image, double it (2048):
         the scale is measured in pixels, and drizzle doubles the pixels.
         Large, faint nebulae need larger scales than star fields. Set the
         four previews around the starting value and compare.

         \note {
            Measured on the same RedCat 51 / IMX571 dual-band master: best
            scale 2048 with 2&times; drizzle, 768&ndash;1024 without drizzle.
            Much larger scales (4096 on that drizzled master) became too
            coarse and left part of the gradient in the image.
         }
      }

      \subsection { \label scale_factors Scale Factors } {

         \center {
            \image[width:520px] images/Part1_ScaleFactors.png
         }

         MGC fits the MARS reference to your image with one scale factor per
         channel. Part 1 offers three settings:

         \definition {
            { Standard (1.0) } { All factors 1.0 (the behaviour before
                              v2.7). }
            { Automatic }   { MGC computes one factor per channel itself,
                              aiming for a model that contains no image
                              structure. About the same run time as
                              Standard. Preview names end in \c _autoSF. }
            { Both (compare) } { Two previews per scale, \c BM_&lt;scale&gt; and
                              \c BM_&lt;scale&gt;_autoSF, to compare in the model
                              window. The warning icon is a reminder that
                              this costs about 60&ndash;70\% more MGC time:
                              the MARS work is done once, but each scale is
                              modelled twice. }
         }

         Automatic is not always better. In tests it gave a flatter
         background on 2&times; drizzled masters, but not on the same master
         without drizzle; the factors themselves also differ between
         drizzled and undrizzled versions of one image. Use \s {Both} when you
         are unsure, and decide in the model window.

         The automatic factors don't depend on the gradient scale, so they
         are the same for every \c _autoSF preview. The console and the
         model window show them together with their \e correlation: how
         clearly MGC could determine each factor. A factor with a correlation
         below 0.75 is marked &#9888; and is uncertain; compare it with the
         1.0 model.
      }

      \subsection { \label choose_model Step 4b --- Choose Background Model } {

         \center {
            \image[width:420px] images/Part1_MGC.png
         }

         When all previews are done, the \s {Choose background model} window
         opens. While it is open, PixInsight is busy: you can't zoom or pan
         the image. Click a model in the list to show its preview. With
         automatic scale factors, the top of the window shows the factors
         and their correlation.

         \definition {
            { Apply }       { Applies the selected model to the main image at
                              once: image &minus; model + median(model). The
                              model used and its scale factors are noted in
                              the FITS history. }
            { Stronger model stretch (same for all models) } { Display only.
                              Shows every model with one stronger stretch,
                              computed from all models together, so you
                              compare like with like. Off: the models use
                              the image's own stretch. Don't judge models
                              with PixInsight's AutoSTF on each preview
                              separately: it stretches every model
                              differently. }
            { Add scale / Run MGC } { If none of the models is good enough,
                              run MGC at another scale. It gets its own new
                              \c BM_ preview with the same settings and
                              appears in the list, ready to compare and
                              apply. With \s {Both}, it adds the 1.0 and the
                              \c _autoSF model. }
            { Remove background model previews after applying } { On by
                              default. }
            { Open MGC tool instead } { Opens the MGC tool with the selected
                              preview's settings, for manual testing. }
            { Close }       { Leaves the image unchanged. }
         }
      }
   }

   \subsection { \label step_gc Step 5 --- GC (GradientCorrection) } {

      Off by default. GC also starts automatically when SPFC or MGC fails,
      for example when the MARS database doesn't cover the target.

      GC deletes the image's existing previews first and runs with two
      presets on its own previews:

      \table[header, unnumbered] {
         { {Preview} {Preset} {Gradient model window} }
         { { \c GC_ }  { GC defaults (protection 0.10 / 0.50): follows the
                         gradient closely, but can leave blotches on
                         nebula-rich fields. } { \c GC_gradient_model } }
         { { \c GC_1 } { Stronger protection (0.05 / 0.70): smoother model
                         that spares nebulae, but may under-correct. }
                       { \c GC_gradient_model1 } }
      }

      The gradient model windows get an Adaptive Auto STF. Differences
      usually show more clearly in the models than in the corrected previews.
      Then the \s {Choose GC result} window opens (step 5b):

      \center {
         \image[width:520px] images/Part1_GC.png
      }

      It works like
      \lref choose_model {Choose background model}: \s {Apply} copies the
      selected preview to the main image, \s {Remove GC previews and model
      windows after applying} is on by default, and \s {Open GC tool
      instead} opens GC with that preview's settings.

      \s {Show gradient models (same stretch for all)} is for display only:
      clicking a result then brings its model window to the front instead of
      its preview, and both models get one shared stretch so they can be
      compared. Unchecked, the previews show again and the model windows get
      their own stretch back. \s {Apply} always uses the corrected preview.

      \note {
         Close old \c GC_gradient_model windows before running GC. Otherwise
         GC numbers the new models differently and they no longer match the
         preview names.
      }
   }

   \subsection { \label step_dbe Step 6 --- DBE (Custom DynamicBackgroundExtraction) } {

      Off by default. Intended for a manual background extraction. The
      DynamicBackgroundExtraction tool opens with preset values and sample
      points around the frame. Adjust, add, move or delete samples, then
      apply.
   }

   \subsection { \label notes1 Notes } {

      \warning {
         If a gradient correction from this script (MGC or GC) is already
         applied, the script warns before it runs: correcting twice can damage
         the image. To try another model, go back one step in History
         Explorer first. The warning follows History Explorer: after undoing
         the correction, it no longer appears.
      }

      The separation and SPFC are skipped automatically when they were
      already applied to the image. These checks follow History Explorer:
      if you go back to a state before plate solving or SPFC, the script
      solves and calibrates again.

      If you undo the image history back to the start, the astrometric
      solution is lost and the next run solves the image again.

      These checks use marks the script stores in the image. They are kept
      when you save the image as XISF and reopen it; other formats, such as
      FITS, don't keep them.
   }
}

